# Compilers and build tools, each one actually building
# something.

shot    as09
for     as09
cap     as09 assembling 6809 source.  It says nothing and writes Motorola
cap     S-records beside the source -- so the proof is the file: four
cap     instructions at $1000, in the format a ROM burner eats.  A 6809
cap     cross-assembler running on a 68000; as0, as1, as4, as5 and as11 are
cap     its cousins for the rest of the 6800 family, and not one of the six
cap     assembles 68k code.
run     cat /dd/tmp/t.a
wait    6
run     as09 /dd/tmp/t.a
wait    10
run     cat /dd/tmp/t.s19
wait    8

shot    bison
cap     GNU bison 1.19 on a two-rule grammar it writes for itself, then
cap     the report -v leaves behind: the rules as bison read them, the
cap     terminals with the rules they appear in, and the numbers it gave
cap     them.  It says nothing at all when it succeeds.
run     echo "%token NUM" > /dd/tmp/b.y
wait    3
run     echo "%%" >> /dd/tmp/b.y
wait    3
run     echo "expr : expr NUM | NUM ;" >> /dd/tmp/b.y
wait    3
run     bison -v /dd/tmp/b.y
wait    15
run     head -n 12 /dd/tmp/b.output 2>/nil
wait    6

shot    flex
cap     flex turning a lexical description into C.
run     echo "%%" > /dd/tmp/l.l
wait    5
run     echo "[0-9]+   { return NUM; }" >> /dd/tmp/l.l
wait    5
run     echo ".        ;" >> /dd/tmp/l.l
wait    5
run     flex /dd/tmp/l.l
wait    8
run     head -n 10 lex.yy.c
wait    5

shot    gcc
for     gcc gcc_cc2 gcc_cccp2 gpp gpp_cc1plus gpp_cccp gpp_collect gcc_cc1 gcc_collect
cap     GCC 1.39 and its passes.  Asked to compile, the driver prints the
cap     preprocessor command it wants to run and then cannot find `cccp':
cap     the passes are installed under a prefix -- gcc2 wants gcc_, gpp
cap     wants gpp_ -- and the driver does not look where they live.
run     gcc -v
wait    4
run     gcc -v -c /dd/SRC/load/load.c
wait    12

shot    gmake
cap     GNU make, and dmake beside it, both saying what they are.
run     gmake -v
wait    5
run     dmake -V
wait    5

shot    m4
for     m4 m4_0.5
cap     m4, the macro processor, expanding a macro from a file.  What ships
cap     as `m4' from 2026-08-28 is the CMDS/REBUILT/m4_0.5 build: the one
cap     that used to be here expanded this same file as `i hr '.
run     echo "define(GREET,Hello \$1 from OS-9)" > /dd/tmp/m.m4
wait    5
run     echo "GREET(reader)" >> /dd/tmp/m.m4
wait    5
run     cat /dd/tmp/m.m4
wait    5
run     m4 < /dd/tmp/m.m4
wait    8

shot    make
cap     One rule, and the two makes on this disk trying to keep it.  The
cap     rule is in the semicolon form because a TAB does not survive being
cap     typed at this terminal -- SCF expands it -- and a Makefile without
cap     its tab is a syntax error.  `make' does not understand that form at
cap     all.  `gmake' does: it echoes the recipe and runs it, and then the
cap     recipe cannot open a file that `cat' reads perfectly well by hand,
cap     and dies on signal 75.  DOC/INDEX already says gmake was added
cap     because make is the broken one; neither of them builds anything
cap     here.
run     echo "hello: /dd/SYS/motd ; cat /dd/SYS/motd > /dd/tmp/hello" > /dd/tmp/Makefile
wait    6
run     cat /dd/tmp/Makefile
wait    5
run     make -f /dd/tmp/Makefile hello
wait    8
run     gmake -f /dd/tmp/Makefile hello
wait    10

shot    makeinfo
for     makeinfo dmake gmake
cap     makeinfo turns Texinfo source into the info files emacs reads.
run     makeinfo --version
wait    8
run     gmake -v
wait    8

shot    p2c
for     p2c
cap     p2c, the Pascal-to-C translator, saying what it is and where its
cap     configuration lives.
run     p2c -?
wait    10

shot    xasm
for     as0 as1 as4 as5 as11
cap     The xasm family: cross-assemblers for the 6800, 6801, 6804, 6805
cap     and 68HC11.  NOT for the 68000, whatever DOC/INDEX said until
cap     2026-08-13 -- and there has never been a 68050.  Each one names its
cap     own processor when it starts.
run     as0
wait    6
run     as11
wait    6
run     as5
wait    6

shot    yacc
cap     yacc reading a grammar and reporting what it built -- the states
cap     and the conflicts, which is how you know a grammar is ambiguous.
run     echo "%token NUM" > /dd/tmp/g.y
wait    2
run     printf "%%%%\nexpr : expr '+' NUM | NUM ;\n" >> /dd/tmp/g.y
wait    3
run     yacc -v /dd/tmp/g.y
wait    8
run     head -n 12 y.output
wait    5

shot    fortran
for     rtf for lnk lnk.org biory creadoc os9lib
cap     The RTF Fortran-77 system -- compiler, driver and linker -- prints
cap     NOTHING until its runtime library is in the module directory.  `load
cap     /dd/CMDS/os9lib' is the whole difference, and this is the before and
cap     the after.  Note the driver has to be called by its full path: `for'
cap     on its own is a bash keyword, and the shell answers before OS-9 can.
run     rtf
wait    6
run     load /dd/CMDS/os9lib
wait    8
run     rtf
wait    8
run     /dd/CMDS/for
wait    6
run     lnk
wait    6